 ## Operating characteristics
-**Time Complexity:** Construction is $O(M^{3/2})$ in the worst case for row displacement compression [S1]. Lookup is $O(1)$ [S2, S3, unofficial].
-**Memory Complexity:** Proportional to the number of non-empty cells plus overhead for domain bits and offsets. It avoids storing empty cells, making it memory-efficient for sparse volumes [S1][S2, unofficial].
-**Performance:** Tested on scenes like "Buddha" (1.09M triangles) and "Dragon" (871.41K triangles), showing competitive memory usage (e.g., 18.67 MB for Buddha) and adequate render times [S2, S3, unofficial].
-**Strengths:** Constant-time lookup, low memory footprint for sparse data, no pointer overhead [S1][S2, unofficial].
-**Limitations:** Static data requirement for perfect hashing (dynamic updates require rehashing or chaining variants) [S1][S2, unofficial]. Collision handling in dynamic variants can degrade performance [S6, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Construction is $O(M^{3/2})$ in the worst case for the displacement algorithm, where $M$ is related to the grid dimensions [S1]. Lookup is $O(1)$ constant time [S1][S2, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Proportional to the number of non-empty cells plus a small overhead for the offset table and domain bits. The hash table size is typically $2 \times$ the number of non-empty elements [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Performance:** Provides adequate render times without severe impact on time-to-image for static scenes [S3, unofficial]. Test results show memory usage scaling with scene complexity (e.g., 18.67 MB for the Buddha scene with 1.09M triangles) [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Perfect hashing eliminates collisions; constant-time access; compact storage for sparse data.
+*   **Limitations:** Static data only (rebuilding required for updates); worst-case construction time can be high; requires careful heuristic for grid dimensions to avoid excessive memory usage [S1][S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Introduced in 1977 by Aho and Ullman for compaction of deterministic finite automata transition diagrams [S1]. Adapted for ray tracing by Lagae and Dutre (2008) to compress sparse 3D matrices and avoid storing empty array cells [S2, S3, unofficial]. It is a foundational technique for sparse voxel grids in modern rendering, including neural radiance fields (Instant-NGP) [S5, unofficial][S9].
+The method of computing a perfect hash function using row displacement compression was originally introduced in 1977 by Aho and Ullman for the compaction of deterministic finite automata transition diagrams [S1]. It was later adapted for sparse matrix compression and 3D grid hashing in computer graphics, notably by Lagae and Dutre (2008) for ray tracing acceleration structures [S1][S2, unofficial].
 
